I don&#x27;t disagree or really have a dog in the fight for three out of your four points, but it&#x27;s unfortunate that WASM gets such a bad rap.  The blobs are large because popular compilers force the semantics of popular languages into the Turing tar pit.  WASM binaries are pretty compact by design:<p><pre><code>    const binary = Uint8Array.fromBase64(
        &quot;AGFzbQEAAAABBgFgAX8BfwMCAQAHBgECeDIAAAoJAQcAIABBAmwL&quot;
    );
    const module = new WebAssembly.Module(binary);
    const instance = new WebAssembly.Instance(module, {});
    console.log(instance.exports.x2(3));
</code></pre>
This is a trivial and useless example at 39 bytes, but a real example could be more compact than comparable minified JavaScript.
